<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zhou dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1046–256 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Zhou dynasty was the longest-ruling royal dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, which overthrew the Shang around 1046 BCE and nominally ruled until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-qin">State of Qin</a> ended it in 256 BCE. Its kings granted lands to kinsmen and allies as hereditary states and justified their rule by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandate-of-heaven">Mandate of Heaven</a>. After the western capital fell in 771 BCE, the Eastern Zhou kings lost real power to the states of the Spring and Autumn and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States periods</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty.jpg" alt="Zhou dynasty" width="318" height="344" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ian Kiu · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zhou_dynasty_1000_BC.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
